biome after death  is fundamentally an ecological question  the necrobiome is defined as the  community of organisms  associated with necromass decomposition  and includes their interactions with the  necromass with each other  and with the surrounding habitat and  ecosystem  this concept applies across a wide array  of decomposing organic materials  including but not limited to plant  fungal  dung or frass or animal necromast types  and can occur in both aquatic and  terrestrial realms  as decay progresses the organic matter  and associated activities of the  microbes facilitate the attraction of  invertebrate and vertebrate  saprophages that directly consume the  necromass  and the microbial communities  critically all of these aspects of the  microbiome are interlinked  and the relative importance of each  component of the necrobiome  and their function will depend on the  intrinsic qualities of decaying  necromass  and the extrinsic controlling  environmental factors such as  temperature  and humidity  there are numerous organisms that are  associated with the necrobiome such as  the microbes  insects and vertebrate scavengers but  for the purposes of this presentation  i will focus only on the microbial  activity that is occurring  after host death  there are three main questions that may  come to mind when considering the human  postmortem microbiome  one what is it two  why do we study it and three  how do we study it  to answer the what is it the postmortem  microbiome  is an emerging field in forensic science  with broad application for death  investigation  think about time since death essentially  it is assessing and determining the  dynamics of the microbiome  after host death in this instance after  human death  why do we study it while the foundation  of forensic microbiology  began in the early 2000s resulting from  bioterrorism threats  the expansion of using the entire  microbial communities  so the microbial organisms their genes  and their gene functions  in a forensic context began in the early  2010s  by better understanding the postmortem  microbial dynamics  we will be able to use that information  and incorporate it  into the techniques that are routinely  used during death investigation  such as ballistics such as trace  evidence  and becoming more routinely used your  digital footprint  also the human postmortem microbiome can  be important  in the context of public health it is an  opportunity for practitioners to be able  to identify  public health concerns such as  meningitis  legionnaires or even coven 19.  the next question is how do we study the  postmortem microbiome  at its simplest we're trying to identify  what microbes are present when are they  present  how abundant are they and what is their  function  these questions can typically be  answered by relying on a handful  of molecular biomarkers that aid in  determining the species that are present  in a community at any given time how  these communities change over time  or environmental conditions and what  their function may be there are  different microbial targets  that are most often used for forensic  purposes  to identify the consortium of microbes  present  this includes amplicon-based sequencing  including the 16s marker for bacteria  and archaea  the its marker for fungi and the ats  marker for micro eukaryotes  but there is currently a push towards  whole genome sequencing for these  complex microbiological samples  which may yield greater microbial  resolution increased accuracy of their  relative abundance  and allow identification of more  bacteria archaea  viruses and eukaryotes additionally  this technology can be used for  assessments of putative functional genes  there has been an interest in  post-mortem microbiomes for forensic  applications  for at least the past 10 years  foundational work began with using  surrogate animal models  primarily swine and mouse carcasses for  characterizing the microbes present  after death  and identifying if a predictable  succession pattern would occur  that could help identify how long that  host animal  had been dead the next step was to use  human bodies  for postmortem microbiome studies  initially  these projects were conducted at  anthropological research facilities  which use donor bodies  excellent work on long-term  decomposition and successional patterns  resulted from these studies and continue  to provide  very useful information  for example successional data can  provide information  about which bacteria are present at what  time  these successional data are important  because they in essence provide a  microbiological clock  for the time since death these long-term  successional studies can go out months  or even years in some instances  having these types of data is important  for forensic practitioners  to narrow down the estimated time range  of death  however the use of human donors at  anthropological facilities has its  limitations  typically there are small sample sizes  due to space limitations  and also the donors are from a selected  group of individuals  and thus may not be representative of  the population  some research groups have incorporated a  survey-based approach  that will use swabs collected during  routine case work  to create a database of human postmortem  microbiomes  the use of collecting samples from  routine casework is exciting because  you have sample opportunities from a  wide swath of the population  and a variety of death circumstances  however the limitations to routine case  work is that you  only are able to sample one body at one  point in time  thus you do not have successional data  on any given body  you can see on the screen there are lots  of different publications that have  resulted from these various approaches  for identifying the dynamics of the  human postmortem microbiome  first i'll talk about how these  postmortem microbial collections are  made  trained personnel at a collaborating  medical examiner's office  collects these samples on a routine  basis  this means that a predetermined number  of body sites  are swagged with individual sterile  cotton tip swabs  the individual swab that's associated  with each body part  is then stored at -20 until further  processing  we have up to 10 different body sites  per case  and the number of swabs that are  collected per case depends on the  circumstances of death  for example some bodies received to the  medical examiner's office  may not have all body sites available  for microbial collections  this means that we will collect as many  microbial samples as possible  but ideally we would like to have all 10  body sites collected  per case additionally  for each of the cases we collect the  following metadata  this type of information is beneficial  because it helps us to develop  models of how we can use the postmortem  microbiome  to identify the time sense death perhaps  the cause and manner of death  and even where an individual may have  died  as you can see on the screen these  metadata covariates are typical of the  information  that's collected during routine death  investigation  this includes data such as the case  number sex  race the date of birth the decedent's  residence  the event location of where the death  occurred the time  and date pronounced dead any additional  notes that may be important based on the  scene  the decomposition description the  estimated postmortem interval or pmi  information regarding the height weight  bmi  and then of course the manner and cause  of death  once these swab samples are in the lab  each individual swab  undergoes a series of steps that  ultimately allows us to identify the  microbiome  this includes taking an individual swab  extracting and quantifying the dna  we then send that dna to a core facility  that specializes  in processing and sequencing dna to  identify  what species are in that sample and what  their function may be  currently high throughput sequencing  technologies are used to obtain  all of the genetic information found  within the biomarkers of interest for  any given sample  in this example i was most interested in  what the 16s  rrna region was because that helps us to  identify  the bacterial and archaea that are  present in each sample  the sequence information is then  processed by bioinformatic and  statistical pipelines to provide an  overview of the microbial communities  and this pipeline may include different  outputs  such as species richness diversity  relative abundance and other  common ecological metrics all of these  data  are then visualized and statistical  analysis are performed  to identify if there are any microbial  communities that are associated with  specific questions we have regarding the  death investigation  now i would like to talk about the  results from a large scale survey  of the human postmortem microbiome that  was conducted  in collaboration with the wayne county  medical examiner's office  this office is located in detroit  michigan so in the southeast region of  the state  in collaboration with the chief medical  examiner dr carl schmidt  we were able to collect swab samples  during routine death investigation  from 188 cases  the demographic of these 188 cases  spanned  sex race age manner of death and cause  of death  i would like to note that all of the  samples collected were from adults  meaning individuals aged 18 or older  different microbial swabs were collected  from different body sites  and the microbial communities were  processed using the previously mentioned  pipelines to result  in some very interesting data  this study was the first of its kind to  employ a large-scale survey  that means the 188 cases were by far the  largest number of cases that had been  used  to identify the human postmortem  microbiome  previous work using the human postmortem  microbiome  had a sample size of less than 50 cases  so ours was almost three times larger  than any of the previously reported data  our data resulted in some interesting  trends  the first question we were interested in  answering using data from the human  postmortem microbiome  was are the microbial communities  different based on the body site  we know in living human hosts each body  site  has its own unique resident of microbial  taxa  it seems simplistic but whether or not  this distinction of microbial  communities  based on the body site persisted after  death was an important question  the first way we analyze those data is  through the use of a principal  coordinates analysis plot which you can  see on the left  the easiest way to interpret a pcoa plot  is by looking at the dots  and their relative position to each  other each of those dots  represents the microbial community from  an individual swab sample  the closer the dots are together means  the microbial communities are more  similar to one another  and the farther the dots are from each  other indicates that the microbial  communities are dissimilar  for this particular data visualization  we were interested in five different  sites on the body  this included the eyes nose ears  mouth and rectum the eyes are the green  triangles  the nose are the blue hexagons the ears  are the dark blue diamonds  the mouth are the purple squares and the  rectum are the red diamonds  based on this pcoa it is clear that the  microbial communities are structured  based on body site we see there is a  clear distinction of the rectal samples  the ear samples and also the mouth  samples  now looking at the right side of the  screen this is an alpha diversity plot  based on the phylogenetic distance of  the different microbial communities that  were present in the samples  on the left column these are all the  samples separated out by body site  that are less than 48 hours post-mortem  the right column indicates that the  samples were collected  after an estimated postmortem interval  of 49 hours  what's interesting to see is that in the  ears  nose eyes and mouth there is a  decrease in diversity that occurs after  that two day mark  this trend is not seen in the rectal  samples so we know that those  communities are more stable for longer  periods of time after death  also based on these swabs we used  predictive and silico  functional analysis this means the  nucleotide sequences  identified in each of the samples were  run through  a separate bioinformatic pipeline to  identify the potential  function of the different sequences  for this heat map the columns represent  the different body sites  and their predicted functional profiles  the brighter yellow colors signifies an  increased detection  in function after 48 hours post-mortem  essentially we're seeing an increase in  this activity approximately two days  after death  based on these results it was very  interesting to identify  that after 48 hours there was an  increase in activity of bacterial  chemotaxis  bacterial motility proteins and also  flagellar assembly  we hypothesized that the increase in  activity from these results  was indicative of the bacterial being  allowed to proliferate  and spread throughout the body because  there was no longer an influence of the  host immune system  keeping that bacterial proliferation in  check  samples collected from the large scale  survey were also  used in antibiotic resistance gene  detection  my colleague dr heather jordan at  mississippi state university  used several different assays across 34  samples  to identify if there were specific  antibiotic resistance genes  these results were surprising because  almost 60 percent of all of the samples  had antibiotic resistance genes detected  using whole genome shotgun sequencing  this antibiotic resistance was confirmed  also through the use of a commercially  available qpcr kit  that was specifically focused on  antibiotic resistance genes  additionally using these data we  collaborated with a public health  geographer  here at michigan state university dr  amber pearson  the focus of this project was to  identify how the human postmortem  microbiome  correlated with the neighborhood blight  and greening efforts that are occurring  throughout the city of detroit  based on these analysis we were able to  identify positive correlations  between light and greening efforts and  particular bacteria of interest  specifically blight had a positive  correlation with potential pathogenic  bacteria such as clostridium species  while green remediation had positive  correlations with potential commensals  such as lactobacillus  i'm excited to continue these different  types of collaborations to further  enhance the knowledge of the human  postmortem microbiome  as of today we have a database of human  postmortem microbiome samples at  michigan state university  this database began in 2014 and  currently we have over 3 000 cases  over 15 000 individual swabs and we have  four different countries that are  participating in the collection process  of the human postmortem microbiome  while we have a lot of samples and  continued to collect we've only  scratched the surface  on the total number of cases and the  total number of  swabs that have been processed and put  through these pipelines to identify the  microbiome  i've recently been awarded funding from  the national institute of justice  that will help us dig deeper into this  postmortem database  in collaboration with dr schmidt dr  jordan and dr eric benbow at michigan  state university  we are excited to move forward on  exploring new microbiome data  from these samples one question that may  seem simple but is very important is how  do we analyze all of these big data in a  forensic context  there are several open source  bioinformatic pipelines that are  available  that are routinely used in human  microbiome projects  the question is how well do those  bioinformatic pipelines work for  forensic applications  and the postmortem microbiome  first the data are run through some  common bioinformatic pipelines  then there are questions answered such  as do we verify the data  do we normalize the data do we keep all  of the raw data  and then we compare the outputs that are  produced by these pipelines to see how  similar or dissimilar the results may be  the results from these analysis comes  from the work of excellent graduate  students  after going through all of the steps  you're left with millions  of reads how do you find useful  information within those millions of  reads  well you look at big data and how it can  be useful in forensics  for one example we can take all of the  sequences that were found in those 188  cases  and identify if there are different body  areas that are going to be most  informative  for answering the questions of when did  a person die  how did they die and where did they die  evaluation and analysis of these data  continues to be ongoing  this results in part because software  continues to be developed  that is more specific to answering the  questions of interest related to  microbiome data  also computational capacity is becoming  much more powerful  more time efficient and less expensive  thus  we may be able to generate larger data  sets and ask  bigger questions that are important for  forensics  the results and information that we  gather from the data sets today  may be slightly different from that  information and results that may be  generated in the future  using newer techniques  we continue to update our analytical  approaches  and increase the power of this data set  adding new samples to it  each time we sequence new samples and  use those updated analytical approaches  as we move forward there needs to be  strenuous validation of the microbial  based biomarkers identified from this  foundational work  once the performance accuracy and error  of these microbial markers have been  rigorously validated  it may help to identify questions that  are important during death investigation  such as how long has an individual been  dead where did they die  and perhaps what is their cause and or  manner of death  additionally public health is in the  forefront of everybody's mind right now  and the postmortem microbiome provides a  unique opportunity  to screen individuals that may not have  otherwise had access to health care  to detect if antibiotic resistance genes  are spreading in unsuspecting  populations  or perhaps to trace the spread and  prevalence of a disease outbreak or  pandemic  there is also a lot of private sector  interest for how the postmortem  microbiome can be used  specifically interest in how to create  useful and user-friendly assays or  algorithms  to provide reliable information  pertaining to death investigation  and finally as we continue to expand our  knowledge of the postmortem microbiome  we need to think beyond adult cases most  of the published literature has focused  on adult cases  however we should also consider  overlooked populations  such as pediatric and infants to  identify  if the postmortem microbiome can aid in  the determination of their cause and  manner of death  with that i want to thank you for your  attention and i also want to thank my  extraordinary collaborators and graduate  students who have all put a  tremendous amount of effort into these  projects  i would also like to thank the funding  sources for supporting this type of work  i'm more than happy to take any  questions however if you have additional  questions at a later point  please feel free to send me a message at  my email listed above  it is p e c h a  l j e m s u  dot e d u thank you again for your  attention and i hope you have a great  day

