eographer  here at michigan state university dr  amber pearson  the focus of this project was to  identify how the human postmortem  microbiome  correlated with the neighborhood blight  and greening efforts that are occurring  throughout the city of detroit  based on these analysis we were able to  identify positive correlations  between light and greening efforts and  particular bacteria of interest  specifically blight had a positive  correlation with potential pathogenic  bacteria such as clostridium species  while green remediation had positive  correlations with potential commensals  such as lactobacillus  i'm excited to continue these different  types of collaborations to further  enhance the knowledge of the human  postmortem microbiome  as of today we have a database of human  postmortem microbiome samples at  michigan state university  this database began in 2014 and  currently we have over 3 000 cases  over 15 000 individual swabs and we have  four different countries that are  participating in the collection process  of the human postmortem microbiome  while we have a lot of samples and  continued to collect we've only  scratched the surface  on the total number of cases and the  total number of  swabs that have been processed and put  through these pipelines to identify the  microbiome  i've recently been awarded funding from  the national institute of justice  that will help us dig deeper into this  postmortem database  in collaboration with dr schmidt dr  jordan and dr eric benbow at michigan  state university  we are excited to move forward on  exploring new microbiome data  from these samples one question that may  seem simple but is very important is how  do we analyze all of these big data in a  forensic context  there are several open source  bioinformatic pipelines that are  available  that are routinely used in human  microbiome projects  the question is how well do those  bioinformatic pipelines work for  forensic applications  and the postmortem microbiome  first the data are run through some  common bioinformatic pipelines  then there are questions answered such  as do we verify the data  do we normalize the data do we keep all  of the raw data  and then we compare the outputs that are  produced by these pipelines to see how  similar or dissimilar the results may be  the results from these analysis comes  from the work of excellent graduate  students  after going through all of the steps  you're left with millions  of reads how do you find useful  information within those millions of  reads  well you look at big data and how it can  be useful in forensics  for one example we can take all of the  sequences that were found in those 188  cases  and identify if there are different body  areas that are going to be most  informative  for answering the questions of when did  a person die  how did they die and where did they die  evaluation and analysis of these data  continues to be ongoing  this results in part because software  continues to be developed  that is more specific to answering the  questions of interest related to  microbiome data  also computational capacity is becoming  much more powerful  more time efficient and less expensive  thus  we may be able to generate larger data  sets and ask  bigger questions that are important for  forensics  the results and information that we  gather from the data sets today  may be slightly different from that  information and results that may be  generated in the future  using newer techniques  we continue to update our analytical  approaches  and increase the power of this data set  adding new samples to it  each time we sequence new samples and  use those updated analytical approaches  as we move forward there needs to be  strenuous validation of the microbial  based biomarkers identified from this  foundational work  once the performance accuracy and error  of these microbial markers have been  rigorously validated  it may help to identify questions that  are important during death investigation  such as how long has an individual been  dead where did they die  and perhaps what is their cause and or  manner of death  additionally public health is in the  forefront of everybody's mind right now  and the postmortem microbiome provides a  unique opportunity  to screen individuals that may not have  otherwise had access to health care  to detect if antibiotic resistance genes  are spreading in unsuspecting  populations  or perhaps to trace the spread and  prevalence of a disease outbreak or  pandemic  there is also a lot of private sector  interest for how the postmortem  microbiome can be used  specifically interest in how to create  useful and user-friendly assays or  algorithms  to provide reliable information  pertaining to death investigation  and finally as we continue to expand our  knowledge of the postmortem microbiome  we need to think beyond adult cases most  of the published literature has focused  on adult cases  however we should also consider  overlooked populations  such as pediatric and infants to  identify  if the postmortem microbiome can aid in  the determination of their cause and  manner of death  with that i want to thank you for your  attention and i also want to thank my  extraordinary collaborators and graduate  students who have all put a  tremendous amount of effort into these  projects  i would also like to thank the funding  sources for supporting this type of work  i'm more than happy to take any  questions however if you have additional  questions at a later point  please feel free to send me a message at  my email listed above  it is p e c h a  l j e m s u  dot e d u thank you again for your  attention and i hope you have a great  day
 

